WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.310
- A couple of years ago,

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:03.410
I visited Silicon Valley in California

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.200
and there I met with a venture capitalist

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.120
and we were talking about
some great startup ideas,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.820
what the market was doing.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.630
And this person shared
that every now and then

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.820
there will be a startup
approaching this VC firm

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.910
and pitching an idea about
a data analytics tool.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.000
And in the end of the day,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.510
it would turn out that what
these people were building

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:29.210
was pretty much just a new
version of Excel pivot tables.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.450
So this little anecdote
hopefully makes clear

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.520
that pivot tables in Excel

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.770
are really, extremely powerful.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:36.620
They are a tool

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.850
that you will be expected
to definitely master

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.320
in any consulting role,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.740
for sure in the big MBB
strategy consulting firms

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.530
like McKinsey, BCG and Bain.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.810
So in this video, I'm going
to talk exactly about that.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.770
And this is pivot tables in Excel.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.410
So welcome to a new coffee
break here on my channel,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.380
Firm Learning.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:55.830
My name is Heinrich, and on my channel,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.010
I want to help you to become successful

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.010
in the first years of your career.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.740
And to help you follow
along in this video,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.710
I will offer the data set in Excel

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.080
that we use to build this,
including the tables,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.620
the results of this video,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.960
as a donor for you, of course, for free.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.630
You can access this via the
link in the video description.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:13.887
Check it out to get the data set,

00:01:13.887 --> 00:01:16.210
and then follow along
while I show you the things

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.280
here in this video.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.090
Let's start by looking at the data set

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.980
that will serve as the
basis for your pivot table.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.360
And you can imagine this to
be the dataset of a retailer.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.390
So data like that in such
a simple, normalized format

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.410
you can commonly obtain as an export

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.890
from all your ERP or
business information systems.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.070
What we have here is in the
first column, the stores.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.020
So as you can see here,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.050
this company has different
stores that it operates.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.290
Every store has their own ID

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.990
that is associated to the store,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.500
and then there are lots of sales reps.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.840
So here we just have the ID of the reps.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.560
You can see that there are, apparently,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.120
about 154 sales reps, like that.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.038
And then the data is
breaking down by month.

00:01:59.038 --> 00:02:00.037
So you have all the months here first,

00:02:00.037 --> 00:02:02.610
and the numeric value from one to 11.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.680
So as you can see here,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.640
this is just data from
January to November.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.670
And then you can see both the net revenue

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.480
that here was generated and also the cost.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.210
So the cost of goods sold, COGS.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.320
Now let's include a pivot table.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.700
And I'll just highlight
it here, the whole table.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.147
And now to access pivot tables,

00:02:21.147 --> 00:02:25.340
you click here on insert and
then you click on pivot tables

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.200
and then you can just go
here on from table range.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.290
And if you click on that,

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.340
it asks first for the table range

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.080
and here it already then
pre-selects the range

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.270
that was selected before.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.103
So as you can see,

00:02:36.103 --> 00:02:38.490
this is exactly the
table that we have here

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.480
until the end here of the sheet.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.840
And then it asks where this
is supposed to be inserted.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.690
So do you wanna have it on a new worksheet

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.140
or on an existing worksheet?

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.000
You can just leave it as it,
and then if you click okay

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.690
you can see that here
it inserted a new tab.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.430
And let's maybe just call it pivot table

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.450
here in the bottom.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.360
Now you can see it looks
something like this

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.840
and here on the right,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.180
you have all the different
variables here of the table.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.610
So for this to work properly,

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.920
it's important that each
column has a column header.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.190
So on the first row of each
column there needs to be,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.780
yeah, some header, some name.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.970
If this is missing,
you might get an error.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.510
And also here, all the
different variables.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.310
So here for New York, for instance,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.920
in every row, it needs to repeat New York.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.080
So what doesn't work is that
maybe only in until here,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.080
only maybe in the first you have New York

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.420
and then you leave all this
empty, this doesn't work.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:30.550
This will give you errors.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.002
This will hinder how
your pivot table works.

00:03:33.002 --> 00:03:34.047
You can see that right now,

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.950
the pivot table is just an
empty field pretty much,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.260
but here on the right,

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.210
you have these four key fields, right?

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.890
You have filters, you have columns,

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.180
you have rows and you have values.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.750
So let's start here
with the values bucket.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.620
The idea is now that you
can just drag and drop

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.130
these variables in these four areas here.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.540
So let's start with the net revenues.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.530
So let's imagine here
in values, net revenue.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:00.707
We just want to have the number.

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:04.010
And this now just gives me
the total revenue number

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:04.843
in the table.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:06.150
And we can just verify that.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.780
So if I just highlight all of that,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.320
we can see it here on the bottom.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:14.320
The sum of this is 6.7998,
and so on, yeah, million Euro,

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.550
which is exactly the number
that we have in here.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.233
Of course, this is not so insightful yet.

00:04:19.233 --> 00:04:21.170
So maybe let's now split it up

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.017
by different variables, right?

00:04:22.017 --> 00:04:23.800
And we can do that here in the rows.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.190
And maybe first one to go
for would be the stores.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.000
Maybe we wanna see how now the
revenue breaks down by store.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.020
And we can just do that by
dragging and dropping here

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.970
the store name to the rows.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:35.070
And now, as you can see,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.520
maybe let me zoom in here a little bit,

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.450
as you can see now, it shows
me the revenues by store.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.210
Of course, we can also format this here

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.670
as an accounting number.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.020
And this, of course, is not great, right,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.410
because now it gives you
already some basic data

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.620
which, of course, you could
also generate from this table

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.090
but it will take you some time, right?

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.380
It would take you like,

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.080
and of course, different
ways of doing that,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.410
but it's not that, really.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.980
Usually the pivot tables are the best ways

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.940
to really create some aggregated data,

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.130
aggregated things that
you can access here.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.410
Now, next, you can use more
than one variable in the row.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.280
So let's say we want to
break up the revenue data

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.770
of the stores also by months.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.080
And we can just then drag as
an additional variable here,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.296
the months in here.

00:05:17.296 --> 00:05:19.690
All right, so now you can see
here January, February, March.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:20.820
Of course, instead of that,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.540
we can also just drag and drop here

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.650
this other variable, which
just gives the numbers.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.400
And surely like this,
probably not that helpful,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.950
so let's take out here the
names and just the numbers,

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:30.783
or the other way.

00:05:30.783 --> 00:05:32.460
Of course, we can also access both.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.480
Let's maybe stick with the names for now.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.590
And now you can see that
it's breaking up the data

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:38.900
by these variables.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.790
You can see that here I can
collapse and expand this

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.260
to then just get the totals.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.220
If I right click, I can
also access this menu

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.710
and, for instance, say
collapse entire field, right,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.280
to collapse them all or then
also expand entire field

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.680
to get them all again.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.480
Now let's also look at some
other variables, right?

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.110
I can pretty much nest in here
as many variables as I want.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.430
Maybe I wanna also look at the sales reps.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.610
And now we can break up
the revenue by store,

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.630
by months and by sales reps.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.260
Maybe let's take out the months again,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.840
just to have the stores
and the sales reps.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.180
The order matters, right?

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.280
If I turn it around,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.240
if I first start with the sales reps

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.870
and then with the store name,

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.470
you can see that it looks like this.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.080
So maybe not that
interesting, but of course,

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.700
you can also just use
little exercises like this

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:26.600
just to get a better feel for the data.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.300
So maybe you are asking yourself

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.640
is one sales rep only working in one store

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.020
or is one sales rep maybe also
working in different stores?

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.510
In more than one store, right?

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:37.450
And if we look at that,

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.890
we can see that for each
of these sales reps,

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.370
there's always only one store listed.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.250
So if ones sales rep ID would,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.490
it would be apparent, would
show up in more than one stores,

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.080
you would have here different rows

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.980
with different stores
nested under the sales rep,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:53.140
which is not the case here.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.500
And now maybe you want to show the months

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.820
but not here in the rows, but
maybe in the columns, right?

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.780
So this is an additional field
that you have available here.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.650
So if we just take the months

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.220
and I just drag it here
in the columns field,

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.170
what you can see is that

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:09.070
instead of breaking the
fields down here in the rows,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.850
I can just show the values
here in the columns,

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.430
which, of course, makes a lot
of sense for continuous data,

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:17.263
like time data.

00:07:17.263 --> 00:07:18.390
So for other variables,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.500
if we want to put the sales
reps here in the columns,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:22.997
this of course gives me a result, right?

00:07:22.997 --> 00:07:26.800
And I can see now all the
sales reps by stores like that.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:28.690
But of course, that's not
really intuitive to read.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.857
So for this, usually you will
rather go with the columns.

00:07:31.857 --> 00:07:33.610
And of course, now I can also do this here

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.860
on the monthly data, as
we can see right here,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:39.360
and then have the stores
breaken down by the sales reps

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.800
and then those data here in the columns.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:42.970
Last but least,

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.680
there's the filter bucket
where you can insert filters.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.650
So let's say we wanna show this data

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.500
but not for all the sales reps,

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.470
but only for some sales reps.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.190
So I can now drag such a variable,

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:57.279
for instance, here the sales
rep IDs into the filters.

00:07:57.279 --> 00:07:59.370
Now you can see that
it added here this row

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:00.640
where I can now click on that.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:01.473
And I'll select that.

00:08:01.473 --> 00:08:02.690
Maybe I just wanna look at this

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.830
maybe just for the sales
rep with the ID number one.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:07.863
And that's apparently a sales rep

00:08:07.863 --> 00:08:09.840
that maybe isn't with the company anymore,

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.310
so let's pick another one,

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.130
maybe with the sales rep number five.

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.430
And here we can see this person

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.450
only worked in the store,
New York, in April and July.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.900
Of course, you can also go
and select multiple items

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.010
by selecting several sales reps,

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:23.560
just here, randomly select some

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.040
and then we can get all the data for that.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.340
Of course, I can do this with
other variables, as well.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.060
Let's take that out and maybe let's say

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.910
that I only wanna show,

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:32.870
maybe let's take that out again,

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.730
I wanna show the revenue
data for the stores,

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.890
but not for all months,

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.000
but only for some months, right?

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.380
So I can go in here and
now just look at the number

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.180
for January or for February, right?

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:47.140
Or maybe I wanna show
it for January to March.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:48.330
What is also often handy

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.380
is that you cannot only report
on the sum of a variable,

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:52.450
like the revenue,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.030
but sometimes you also wanna look into

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.870
some other types of reports here, right?

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.040
So then you right click
on a variable here,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.730
like the sum of the revenue.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.560
You click on value field settings,

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.030
and now you can see that you can report

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.090
all different types of things.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:08.020
So count, average, max, min,
product and so on and so forth.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.270
So I think in this instance,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.640
this doesn't make too much sense.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.590
What we could, for instance,
maybe do is look at the max.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.940
And now it reports the
highest revenue figure

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:19.320
that it finds for the store and the table.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.380
And if you remember how
the table is set up,

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.630
probably what this would mean is that

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:26.560
this number represents the
highest monthly revenue

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:30.370
that one sales rep created in this store.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:31.910
So again, this depends a bit on how here

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:33.260
the base table is set up.

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.130
Often the sum, though,
will be the go-to format

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.520
that you want to go for, indeed.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.110
Now let's look into some
layout-ing considerations.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.550
So maybe this example, again,

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.810
where you had the stores
and then the month,

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:47.140
not in the filters, but here in the rows.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.690
So of course, while this is a great view

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:52.150
sometimes you also want to have
such a data set as an output

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.180
than in a normalized table format,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.780
because we can see here
that now the column A

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.800
contains mixed information, right?

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.975
It contains both the name of the store

00:10:00.975 --> 00:10:03.610
and also the ID of the month,

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.390
which is not always favorable

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.530
if then you want to do some
other things with the data.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:09.020
So what you can do to fix it is

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:10.610
right click here on the table

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.770
and then go on pivot table options.

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.700
And then under display,
you find here the option

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:19.560
classical pivot table layout,

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.710
enables dragging of fields and so on.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.550
So usually I like to have this activated

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:25.740
because what this is doing,

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:28.120
it separates all the different information

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:29.340
in different columns,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.173
which is what you need,

00:10:30.173 --> 00:10:32.300
and then in the end
with such a pivot table

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:35.630
you want to create a
normalized table format again,

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.120
which can be helpful, for instance,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.670
with building dashboards.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.440
So if you watch my other video

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.910
on creating dashboards in Excel,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.280
you know what I'm talking about.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:44.960
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.463
This technique is really
helpful to, for instance,

00:10:46.463 --> 00:10:48.480
then with some formulas and so on

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.290
build some quite dynamic dashboards.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.490
Check out the video if you
wanna learn more about that.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.580
You can also do things like deleting

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.630
or taking out here the subtotals.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:57.580
So we can see that here we have

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.350
the subtotal for the store name.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:03.030
We can take it out, of course,
inserting again, if we want.

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:03.863
And then, again,

00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:06.310
for the sake of having
normalized, clean tables,

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:07.343
often what you need to do is

00:11:07.343 --> 00:11:09.620
that you want to repeat
the labeling, right?

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.060
So you don't want the store name

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.150
only to appear in the first row,

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.680
but you want it to appear in all the rows,

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.620
again, in order to have a
nice, normalized table format.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:19.580
So if you wanna do that,

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.670
just here right click,
click field settings,

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.980
and then you can do
here in layout and print

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.320
to go on repeat item labels.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:28.860
You need to tick the check mark

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.490
and then you will have it
repeated throughout the table.

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.280
Again, you might want then also to

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:34.750
take out the subtotal again

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:39.310
to have a very clean,
normalized table to work with.

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:40.200
So let's continue

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.000
and let's say we might want
to do some more, right?

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.700
We not only want to work
here with the revenue data,

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.700
but we also wanna show
some margin information

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:52.400
by store and by month,

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.720
or maybe let's not do it by
month, but by sales rep, right?

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.660
So we might want to compare
the different margins

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.290
that the sales reps have available here.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:04.100
So let's take out the
filters to show all the reps

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.893
in all the stores.

00:12:05.893 --> 00:12:06.740
And, of course,

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:09.570
the variable that we have
available here are the cost.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:13.680
So I can now drag the cost
also here in the values area

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.520
to now show both the net
revenue and the cost.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:17.510
But now one thing,

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.620
maybe let's start first
without the reps to simplify,

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.730
now here have the revenue
and the cost data.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:24.430
One thing that, of course,
typically you would like to do

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:27.930
is you would now take the
revenue and subtract the cost

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.920
in order to get to the margin,

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:31.720
to the gross margin.

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:32.553
The problem is that

00:12:32.553 --> 00:12:35.010
you cannot really work
like this with pivot tables

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.210
because if you just copy that over,

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:40.430
as you can see, it does
not really then go here

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.790
and then adjust the formula accordingly.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:43.940
So that doesn't work.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.597
There are some other ways to
like, you know, make it work.

00:12:46.597 --> 00:12:48.970
You know, let's not go too much into that.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:50.600
Of course, another alternative would be

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.470
you can just take the table

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.807
and you can copy just the values, right?

00:12:54.807 --> 00:12:56.687
So you can copy the values
and then, of course,

00:12:56.687 --> 00:12:58.980
you just have the data you
have extracted next to you.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.320
You can, of course, also format this

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:02.760
in an accounting format.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.620
And then, of course, you
can do whatever you like.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.480
So you can do the same calculations,

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.490
and then, of course, also the
style of working with formulas

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:10.800
does work to make that work.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.610
But that's also not the real
way how these pivot tables

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.080
are intended to work because, of course,

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.620
now if you change anything here,

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.200
this doesn't adjust automatically.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:19.120
But sometimes this can be a little hack

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.460
to a very quick and straightforward way

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:22.920
to do your own calculations.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.800
So instead, what you can do

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.146
to really make this work in pivot tables

00:13:27.146 --> 00:13:31.540
is to use something that is
called calculated fields, right?

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:34.196
So if you here click on
the pivot table analyze,

00:13:34.196 --> 00:13:36.920
you need to highlight the pivot
table for this to show up,

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:38.820
you go on fields, items and sets,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.010
you go on calculated fields.

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:43.530
Now you can just create
your own additional fields.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.380
So let's say we want to
have the gross margin

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.140
and let's first start
in absolute, so in Euro.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.200
And in order to that here,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.500
it gives me all the fields

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:52.990
that are loaded into the pivot table

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.140
that I can now work with.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:56.950
And of course, the margin
is the net revenue.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.450
So click here on insert field,

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:00.680
and then you can see that
it's added to the formula.

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:03.580
And I'll press minus and
now here, press COGS.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.730
So cost of good sold as an additional one.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.611
And now I add this and now you can see

00:14:07.611 --> 00:14:09.913
that I have here the gross
margin and percent available.

00:14:09.913 --> 00:14:11.900
And now let's do once more, right?

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:13.616
Let's even add an additional field.

00:14:13.616 --> 00:14:15.230
Let's now say we want to have

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:17.870
the gross margin in percentage.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.580
And, of course, this would be then

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:22.230
the gross margin absolute
that we just added

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.890
divided by the net revenue,

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.010
insert to now give us
this additional field.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.222
Now we press okay.

00:14:29.222 --> 00:14:31.110
And now you can see that
it already added that here

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.070
to the table.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.610
Of course, here, the percentage,

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:37.200
we will likely want to form
it as a percentage, indeed,

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.407
to make it look like something like this.

00:14:38.407 --> 00:14:41.120
I think this is already
first interesting analysis

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.740
to now see how different
margins of these stores

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:44.960
compare to each other.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:45.910
And the great thing is that

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:48.140
as soon as you have added those variables

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:49.160
as calculated fields,

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:50.670
you can do all the things we did

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.160
with the pivot table already, right?

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:53.820
So you can add them to the tables,

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:55.500
you can then also drill down with it

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.390
with all the other additional variables.

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.223
So maybe, you know,

00:14:58.223 --> 00:14:59.690
let's just look at the percentage figures,

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.790
then we probably also don't
need the COGS anymore.

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:03.720
Let's say we want to break this down

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:05.800
by the different sales reps.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:08.080
So here by the different
sales reps of the stores

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.521
so we can see what their revenue is.

00:15:09.521 --> 00:15:12.220
We can see what their margin is,

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:13.460
the non-average they created.

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:14.670
Maybe we don't need the stores,

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:15.973
we just have the sales reps here.

00:15:15.973 --> 00:15:17.730
And, of course, you can also do things

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.820
like sorting this whole
thing by the margin.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.560
So here, let's sort by the
margin from largest to smallest.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.740
And, of course, here now we
have some funny data in here.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.280
So sales reps who probably
didn't do a lot of revenue,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:30.770
maybe just were some assistants

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:32.170
working there for a couple of days,

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:33.910
but here we can see
that there are some reps

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:35.360
which also more significant results.

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:38.300
So this person was doing more
than 30,000 Euro in revenue

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.700
and had the margin of 64%.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.110
So there are reps like that,
that are doing quite well.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:43.943
And then of course,

00:15:43.943 --> 00:15:46.230
we can now go down to
the bottom of the table

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:47.494
where we have margins,

00:15:47.494 --> 00:15:50.020
where we have reps who
are doing 60,000 Euro

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.257
and have a really low margin, right?

00:15:52.257 --> 00:15:54.090
And this, of course, would be interesting,

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.080
conducts a basic analyses like this,

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:57.900
have an understanding of the data

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.750
and then to work with this in this way.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:01.960
But that's another thing
that is very powerful

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.730
that you can do with pivot tables.

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:06.810
And this is to use them to
create dashboards in Excel,

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.890
to create dynamic dashboards in Excel.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:11.670
So to make them work,
let's look at an example.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:13.760
Let's maybe work with the store names.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.580
So let's imagine you
wanna make a dashboard

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.510
that helps me to slice into
revenue and margin data

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:19.343
for the store.

00:16:19.343 --> 00:16:21.810
So let's add here the
revenue and the margins.

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:23.920
And the first thing in order to move there

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:25.220
that's already pretty cool

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:28.640
is if you here click
on pivot table analyze,

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:32.274
there is the option
here to insert slicers.

00:16:32.274 --> 00:16:33.530
All right, so you can insert slicers.

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:36.710
Now it asks you what type
of slicers you want to have.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:40.820
So let's say we want to
have slicers for the months

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.630
and maybe also for the store names.

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:45.530
So let's add both of them.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:48.520
And you can see that now it
added these additional panels

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:50.730
that you can here freely
position in your table.

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:53.590
And what this is doing
now just on the table

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:55.290
that you can now select, for instance,

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:57.479
this data just for January

00:16:57.479 --> 00:17:00.300
or just for February or just for March.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.460
So at the end of the day,
it's nothing else but filters.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:04.750
You can also tick several of them,

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:07.000
but it's a bit of a more visual way.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.570
And this can be then very easily
used as an input mechanism

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:15.060
for the dashboards that
you want to create,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:16.500
because the next thing that you can do

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:17.980
is also to insert charts.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.240
So if you click here on the pivot table,

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:21.660
pivot table analyze,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.760
it provides you the opportunity to insert

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.890
a pivot chart, as it's called.

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:26.723
And maybe let's just here

00:17:26.723 --> 00:17:29.080
go for the standard clustered columns.

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:30.720
So here by store,

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.900
it gives me the net revenue
and the gross margin.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:35.750
Of course, you can go with
any other chart, as well.

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.700
And it's a special type of chart

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.260
that automatically adjusts the content

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:44.720
to all the things we have
shown here in the pivot table,

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.050
because now what you can do is that

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:48.970
if you here now change this,

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:52.720
it automatically changes all the contents

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:53.553
of your stores as well.

00:17:53.553 --> 00:17:55.710
And of course you can
highlight both, right?

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:59.760
You can say, let me just show
the data for Berlin for May

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:01.280
or for April or for March.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:02.730
Or maybe I want to have different stores.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.500
So Berlin, Dusseldorf and London.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:07.220
And this is really the core

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:08.940
of how you would build dashboards.

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.480
So if you would wanna make a
great dashboard out of that,

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:14.550
you could now create all types
of pivot tables like that.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.390
Often, then you do a distinct tab

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:17.830
where you hide all your pivot tables

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:20.170
and then you have an output tab

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.107
where you have all kinds
of charts like this.

00:18:22.107 --> 00:18:24.450
And then, of course, you
can format the charts,

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.070
make them look a little bit better.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.980
You can also format these slicers

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.420
to make them look really
cool, really great.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.520
So I don't want to go
into detail here too much

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.350
because, of course, you
can just really go like

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:35.700
spend lots of time talking about this,

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:37.920
but then you have some
controls for the users

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.890
who can just drill down here in the data,

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:41.680
select the dates, select the stores.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:43.240
And this is really a powerful way

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.470
on how you can enable your users to,

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:47.120
in a very intuitive way,

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:48.750
with the help of pivot tables,

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.900
drill down into your data sets.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.130
So there are different
ways of building dashboards

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.510
with Excel pivot tables.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:56.750
For sure, is one very effective of them.

